As a Technical Lead, I can tell you that when a Kafka message is successfully published (which your logs probably show) but the consumer completely ignores it or crashes silently, 95% of the time it is a Serialization or Type Header mismatch.
Looking closely at your code, I see the classic Double Serialization Trap that happens often in Spring Boot Kafka.
The Problem
In your handleLhoApproval method, you are manually converting your Map into a JSON String:
String finalPayloadString = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(customKafkaPayload);

Then you pass that String to your KafkaTemplate<String, Object>.
If your Spring Boot Kafka configuration is set up to use a JsonSerializer for the values (which is standard for <String, Object> templates), Spring will serialize your String again.
Your consumer expects this:
{"requestId": 123, "processId": 12}
But because of double serialization, Kafka actually sends it wrapped in extra quotes with escaped characters, like this:
"{\"requestId\": 123, \"processId\": 12}"
When your consumer tries to read this, it fails to parse it as a valid JSON object and either throws a silent deserialization exception or drops the message.
The Fix
Stop manually converting the payload to a String! Pass the actual Map (or a DTO) directly to the KafkaEventPublisherService, and let Spring Kafka's JsonSerializer do the work.
Here is how to update both files to fix the communication gap:
1. Update LhoTransferStrategy (Remove manual JSON conversion)
    private void handleLhoApproval(CommonReq request) throws JsonProcessingException {
        log.info("Constructing tailored Kafka payload for targetId: {}", request.getTargetId());

        JsonNode originalPayload = objectMapper.readTree(request.getPayload());
        Long processRunId = originalPayload.has("processRunId") ? originalPayload.get("processRunId").asLong() : null;

        Map<String, Object> customKafkaPayload = new HashMap<>();
        customKafkaPayload.put("requestId" , request.getId());
        customKafkaPayload.put("processRunId", processRunId);
        customKafkaPayload.put("processId", 12);
        customKafkaPayload.put("user", request.getExecutorId());
        customKafkaPayload.put("remarks", request.getExecutorRemarks());

        // REMOVED: objectMapper.writeValueAsString(...)
        
        // Fire the event to Kafka - Pass the MAP directly!
        kafkaEventPublisherService.publishLhoTransferEvent(request.getTargetId(), request.getId(), customKafkaPayload);

        request.setReqStatus(RequestStatus.QUEUE);
        request.setExecutionRemarks("Event sent to Kafka. Request moved to QUEUE.");

        log.info("Batch {} processed and placed in QUEUE successfully.", request.getTargetId());
    }

2. Update KafkaEventPublisherService (Accept Object instead of String)
package com.tcs.fincore.CommonRequestService.service.strategy;

import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.SendResult;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

@Slf4j
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class KafkaEventPublisherService {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, Object> kafkaTemplate;

    @Value("${app.kafka.topics.lho-transfer:trigger-events}")
    private String lhoTopic;

    // CHANGED: Accept 'Object' instead of 'String' for the payload
    public void publishLhoTransferEvent(String targetId, Long requestId, Object payload) {
        log.info("Attempting to publish LHO Transfer event for targetId: {} to topic: {}", targetId, lhoTopic);

        // 1. Create a ProducerRecord using the Object payload
        ProducerRecord<String, Object> record = new ProducerRecord<>(lhoTopic, targetId, payload);

        // 2. Add your custom header to the record
        record.headers().add("requestId", String.valueOf(requestId).getBytes());

        // 3. Send it once
        CompletableFuture<SendResult<String, Object>> future = kafkaTemplate.send(record);

        future.whenComplete((result, exception) -> {
            if (exception == null) {
                log.info("Successfully published! Target ID: {} | Topic: {} | Offset: {}",
                        targetId, lhoTopic, result.getRecordMetadata().offset());
            } else {
                log.error("CRITICAL: Failed to publish for Target ID: {}. Error: {}",
                        targetId, exception.getMessage());
            }
        });
    }
}

Next Debugging Steps (If it still fails after this fix):
If changing this still doesn't trigger your consumer, check these two things on the consumer side:
 * Check Consumer Logs for Error: Is the consumer throwing a ValueDeserializationException? If so, the consumer might be strictly configured to only accept String formats, in which case we might need to change your KafkaTemplate to <String, String> and revert to manual string conversion.
 * Topic Name: Double-check that the consumer is definitely listening to trigger-events (or whatever ${app.kafka.topics.lho-transfer} resolves to).
Try passing the Map object directly first—that solves this issue the vast majority of the time!
